items.change:
1. PlayerFactory.buildLive (PlayerFactory.kt:324-332): add .setDetachSurfaceTimeoutMs(500) and keep the default 500 ms release timeout. Live players only; films are unchanged. This bounds the waits Media3 makes on the main thread: surfaceDestroyed when the surface is hidden, and attach() switching surfaces.
2. One shared close step, ExoPlayer.letGo(listener): Long, in DecoderHealth.kt. It is used by PlayerHost.closeStream (543-570), PlayerHost.playerFor's old-player swap (577-584) and MultiviewPlayer.releasePlayer (181-194). Its order:
   (a) p.removeListener(listener) first, so a timeout raised while closing can never start a recovery or leave a stray retry;
   (b) the caller removes retry, stallCheck and pictureCheck;
   (c) NO clearVideoSurfaceView. Media3 1.8.0's release() releases the codec and then removes the surface callbacks itself (ExoPlayerImpl.release bytecode), so the clear only added a codec round trip (setOutputSurface to the placeholder) and a 2000 ms wait;
   (d) t0 = uptimeMillis; p.release(); took = uptimeMillis - t0;
   (e) the caller removes retry again;
   (f) if took >= 450 ms: DecoderHealth.noteSlowRelease(took) and log 'Live: the player took N ms to let go; its video decoder may be stuck'.
3. PlayerHost.attach (207-212) and MultiviewPlayer.attach (117-122): skip clearVideoSurfaceView on the old surface while DecoderHealth.stuck.
4. In MultiviewPlayer.open, release the old player before resetting the retry state, so an old player's late error can't hit the new one.
5. Correct the comments at PlayerHost.kt:546 and MultiviewPlayer.kt:183 ('a stopped player still holds its decoders' isn't true in 1.8.0; release is still kept for the playback thread and buffer).

